<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William I of the Netherlands</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King William I of the Netherlands · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William I was the first King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, reigning from 1815 to 1840. A son of William V, the last stadtholder, and great-grandson of William IV of Orange-Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Diez</a>, he ruled the United Kingdom of the Netherlands, including present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. He promoted trade, canals and industry, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> of 1830 split his kingdom, and he abdicated in 1840.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-i-of-the-netherlands.jpg" alt="William I of the Netherlands" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Paelinck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_I_of_the_Netherlands.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born on 24 August 1772, the son of the last stadtholder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-v-prince-of-orange">William V, Prince of Orange</a>, and Wilhelmina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In 1791 he married his cousin Wilhelmina of Prussia, daughter of King Frederick William II. As commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> field army he fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> campaign of 1793–95 against the French, but the Batavian Revolution forced the family into exile in 1795. In 1803 he received the Principality of Nassau-Orange-Fulda as compensation, and he lost it in 1806 after fighting on the Prussian side at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>–Auerstedt. In 1809 he served with the Austrian army and was wounded at Wagram.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, a provisional government invited William back. He landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheveningen">Scheveningen</a> on 30 November 1813, refused the title of king and proclaimed himself Sovereign Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> also gave him the former Austrian Netherlands, and on 16 March 1815 he proclaimed the kingdom, urged by the powers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>. He later gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> in exchange for ceding his German lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The constitution gave him near-absolute powers, with ministers responsible only to him. He was nicknamed the King-Merchant for his trade policies. In 1817 he founded the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and Liège, and in 1822 the Algemeene Nederlandsche Maatschappij.</p><p>The constitution divided the 110 seats of the Second Chamber equally between north and south, though the south had 3.5 million people against 2 million in the north. William, a strong supporter of the Reformed Church, imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as the official language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and required schools to teach the Reformed faith and the Dutch language. Many in the Catholic south resented this, and also felt economically disadvantaged. After riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in August 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> followed. He sent his sons to invade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-days-campaign">Ten Days' Campaign</a> the next year, but French intervention was threatened, and he fought on until 1839, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> dissolved the united kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William disapproved of the constitutional changes of 1840, which introduced ministerial responsibility, and he grieved the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. He also intended to marry Henrietta d'Oultremont, a Roman Catholic. He abdicated on 7 October 1840 in favour of his eldest son, William II. He spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he died on 12 December 1843 at the age of 71. A new national anthem, Wien Neêrlands Bloed, replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a> during his reign and remained in place until 1932.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William I of the Netherlands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great powers raised it to a grand duchy and gave it to <b>William I of the Netherlands</b> as compensation for the hereditary Nassau lands in Germany that his house surrendered to Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Secularised in 1802, Fulda went to <b>William Frederick of Nassau-Orange</b>, then to French control and the Grand Duchy of Frankfurt.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-fulda">Prince-Abbey of Fulda</a></li><li><blockquote>William IV's grandson became <b>King William I of the Netherlands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-nassau-diez">Principality of Nassau-Diez</a></li><li><blockquote>A provisional government led by Gijsbert Karel van Hogendorp invited <b>William Frederick</b>, son of the last stadtholder, to return.</blockquote><a href="/state/sovereign-principality-of-the-united-netherlands">Sovereign Principality of the United Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.08,4.31&amp;year=1818" aria-label="The Hague on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William I of the Netherlands"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 5668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5226 5668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.08,4.31&amp;year=1818">What was here in 1818? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
